         <td style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 10pt;" background="/images/bg_heading_green_01.jpg"><center><b>The Phoenix Chronicles
: The Direction of Power
 by Eshkin of Prexus
</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
		 <td>                            
	                                   

<P>&#9;To Erudin…</P><br><P>&#9;It had been so long since I had traveled with anyone else, since Surefall Glade really, that I had forgotten how it made the miles go so quickly.  The Jaggedpine rangers are known for the pace they can set,   but Kheldan was a tough travel partner and we never wanted for interesting things to talk about.  </P><br><P>He told me all about the power of his animal totems:  The wolf, which embodied speed and endurance, the bear and the oxen, which embodied toughness, and the cat, which embodied agility.  There were other totems as well...that of ice and of fire, and of disease and of poison.  These last two he referred to as his "darker mana".</P><br><P>&#9;I has always thought of the Shaman of the North as religious figures, just as the priests of Rodcet Nife, or of Tunare.  But it was not so.  Through focus and meditation the shaman made himself--or another--one with these totems.  Imbuing the spirit of the nature totem into himself and drawing on its power.</P><br><P>&#9;Though I was raised as a child of Tunare, I could see the wisdom in it.  To this day, the Shamans of the north are the only "holy men" who, taken as a group, I respect.  Others claim their faith to be right while all others were wrong, or weak, or evil.  The Shamans of the Six hammers made no such assumptions.  Their powers were simply powers, drawn from the ancient nature of Norrath.  How they were used, was strictly the decision of the individual.  </P><br><P>&quot;To the Sprits, power is merely power,&quot; Khel told me, &quot;It is its direction that changes it into good or evil.&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;It was a stirring revelation and I thought about it long after Kheldan had retired to his bedroll for the night.  I watched the smoke from my smoke stick whipped away in the cold night breeze as pondered it.  There was truth to it.  But Tunare was  truth to me also.  Surely there could not be two truths, unless there was something in the way it all fit together that I had not yet seen.</P><br><P>&#9;<I>Power is merely power…</P><br></I><P>Looking back, it was an important bit of thinking, as it made the way ready for other things in the future—for alliances and friendships that would make things possible for us; things that others could not dream. </P><DIR><br><DIR><br><br><P>It was important thinking.</P></DIR><br></DIR><br><br><P>&#9;But we quickly ran out of time for thinking.</P><br><P>&#9;The miles down from the peaks went quickly, and we gave the territory of the gnolls of Blackburrow a wide berth.</P><br><P>&#9;It was spring, my favorite of seasons.  The snowmelt started in the peaks as a trickle, and as we followed it back into the lowlands, it became a stream, and then a torrent of rushing streams cascading down the mountains and hills, in the cold morning sun, in their rush to the sea.</P><br><P>&#9;The air grew quickly warmer as we descended, and in short order, we found ourselves on the rocky highlands above Qeynos hills.</P><br><P>&#9;It was here that we spied the first sign of trouble.</P><br><P>&#9;Kheldan’s bright blue eyes were always wandering, taking in all the newness of the lowlands so strange to him.  In the peaks there is very little underbrush, and the twisted, grasping roots of the trees are well buried beneath the snow.  </P><br><P>Not so down here.</P><br><P>As such, Khel would frequently lose his footing and crash to the ground with a loud windy grunt.  Sometimes, on the slopes, he would fall so hard that I feared him injured—especially when he would roll clamorously down through the brush and rocks.  He never complained.  His response to my concerned look was always the same.</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;I’m alright!  I’m ok!&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;This time, as we descended the last steep slope into the hills, his eyes were blithely fixed on a bright red cardinal when his foot found a hole.  </P><br><P>He topped off the uncertain path like a felled tree.  He rolled and slid down the rocky slope, into a stream.  He then washed down the swollen flume of the stream until it dumped him off a twenty foot waterfall.  </P><br><P>I could hear him as he disappeared over the lip of the waterfall, calling back to me, &quot;I’m alright…&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;Quickly I threaded the rocks down to the pool at the base of the falls—as quickly as I could on the slippery slope.  By the time I got there he was already out of the water—his furs soaked and heavy, and his thick mane of hair matted to his head.  He looked like a great drowned bear.</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;…I’m ok…&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;I couldn’t stop myself from laughing.  &quot;Tunare is not as forgiving of a lazy step, Kheldan.  Your animal gods favor you.  Had it been me, I’d have broken my legs!&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;The Wolves of the North are tougher that that, m’friend.  But it helped tha’ the bottom was so soft.&quot;  The last he said perplexed, wiping the water from his face and gazing into the pool intently.</P><br><P>&#9;I looked at him and then to the water.  It was more than a joke.  </P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Soft?&quot; I asked, wading in to my hips, I felt first with my feet, &quot;How soft?&quot;  My boot nudged something under the cold, frothing  water.  Something with a consistency that, in the dark corners of my mind, I had feared it would have.</P><br><P>&#9;Together, Kheldan and I dragged the corpse from the water.  It was the fresh corpse of a gnoll.  The cold of the stream had kept it from decomposing.  It had been there perhaps two days, not more than three.  I knelt by the corpse as Khel stood straight.  Black of fur…and wearing the rags of Blackburrow.  Heavy rocks were tied around its waist, to keep it weighted to the bottom of the pool.  Its chest and throat were pierced with arrows.</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Esh, there are more in here…&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;All told, we dragged four gnoll corpses from the stream.  All weighted, some slashed to death with swords or axes—some fletched with arrows.</P><br><P>&#9;The arrows were of a strange make…longer and more delicate than I had seen before, and their heads needlelike at the point.  A piercer arrow...meant to punch through plate and dig through chain.  Very expensive.  </P><br><P>A war arrow, not they type of thing the fletchers of the Jaggedpine would make.</P><br><P>&#9;Kheldan looked puzzled as I crouched over the bodies, turning the arrow over between my fingers.  &quot;Ok, so why would anyone want ta sink the gnolls in the stream?  Why na just kill em and leave ‘em lay?  They’re just brutes.&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Not all of them are, Khel…&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Well, brutes or no,  ‘tis mighty strange thing to do…&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;What was this edginess that had suddenly sprung up in me?  Something here…Something that perhaps I had noticed in the back of my mind, but not fully understood.  What was it?</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;They didn’t want the bodies found.&quot;  I said.</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Why not?  Who cares if ya kill a few gnolls?&quot;</P><br><P>&#9;&quot;Other gnolls, mostly...&quot;  For the first time, I looked about the ground around the pool.  There were tracks…not all of them ours…but they had been brushed—barely visible.  And a few broken twigs on the surrounding bushes.  Whoever was here had cleaned up after themselves well.</P><br><P>&#9;Khel was examining the corpses now.  It was apparent that the sight of the dead did not trouble him.  Of course it wouldn’t.  As a shaman of the Six Hammers he was a caretaker and a solace to the sick and the dying of Halas.  He was no stranger to death.  &quot;This one was hit in the head.  The other cut up, and that other one…was shot.  So two men…likely three&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Aye, and not of the Jaggedpine Treefolk either.&quot; I said.  &quot;These bodies would poison the stream.  No ranger or druid of Surefall would dispose of them so.&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Looks like they beat this last one here a bit.&quot;  Khel pointed to the feet of the last corpse.  Its bones were broken and smashed, and stuck through the skin.  There were also great gashes in its shins…showing the white of the bone through the fur and flesh.  </P><br><P>&quot;What kind of weapon would make wounds like that?&quot;  I wondered aloud, turning my focus again to the brush, for more signs of the hunting party.</P><br><P>&quot;No weapon, my friend.  Just a sharp stone.&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Hmm?&quot;  I did not understand. &quot;So our gnoll hunters are one archer, a swordsman, possibly a footman with a mace or warhammer, and a caveman wielding a sharp stone?&quot;</P><br><P>Khel shook his head, a strange, sad half smile tugging at the edges of his moustache, &quot;Ya don’ get it, do ya?&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Uhh….no.&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;They tortured the brute, Esh.&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Tortured?&quot;  the thought of it made me sick.  The brief image of Layla, hanging in bloody rags from the ceiling of our home flashed across my mind.  &quot;Why?&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;For pleasure…or to get him to talk.&quot;</P><br><P>&quot;Talk?  About what?  Gnolls don’t have  a lot of secrets…&quot;</P><br><br><P>Realization flashed across my mind like a lightning bolt.</P><br><P>Well, they did have <B><I>one</B></I> secret, at least.</P>         <br>
